  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "When editing a Looks preset and pressing Cancel, a confirmation dialogue appears with two options: SAVE and DISCARD. This dialogue has a UX issue that can cause users to unexpectedly lose their unsaved work.\n\nThe core problem is that there is no way to return to editing from this dialogue. If a user pressed Cancel by mistake and wants to resume their work, neither SAVE nor DISCARD serves that intent. The only visible escape seems to be the × button on the dialogue — but it behaves identically to DISCARD, silently destroying all unsaved changes. A user who instinctively closes the dialogue with ×, expecting to go back to the editor, will lose everything with no warning.\n\nThe SAVE option is also arguably unnecessary here. If the user intended to save, they would have used the Save button before cancelling. Its presence adds visual noise without addressing the actual missing case.\n\nThe dialogue should be restructured as follows:\n\n- Discard: exit the editor without saving\n\n- Go Back: return to the editor and resume editing\n\nThe × button should map to Go Back, as closing a dialogue is conventionally understood as dismissing it without taking a destructive action. Go Back should also be the default-focused option, since accidental cancellation is the most common scenario that triggers this dialogue.",
